References: <ct4jh1MQKOw.0Kcs-91rBAAJ@blavaan.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/blavaan/c/ct4jh1MQKOw/m/DQv1oXHGBAAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

> I could run a Bayesian CFA with that database and use the posterior distributions as priors

This should be mathematically equivalent to using a noninformative prior and fitting the model to all N = 52 + 44 = 96 observations.  So you could just combine the samples.

Terrence D. Jorgensen
Postdoctoral Researcher, Methods and Statistics
Research Institute for Child Development and Education, the University of Amsterdam
UvA web page: http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen

